CORE
script
--show-parse-tree
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^dialect bash$
^    word lit".+#!/bin/bash"$
--
^failed to figure out type of file
--
A UTF-8 byte order mark before "#!" does not stop the file from being
identified as a shell script, and the #! line still selects the dialect.
The shell itself reads the byte order mark as part of the first word, so
the first line is a command named "<BOM>#!/bin/bash" rather than a
comment, as in bash and dash.
